    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-114345",
      "knowledge_point_id": "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$",
